MOF-Co Materials for Catalytic Alkene Hydrogenation with Unprecendently High Activity. Upon treatment with \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\), MOF-Co materials became highly active catalysts for the hydrogenation of a range of olefins at room temperature (Fig 3a and Table 1). Mono-substituted alkenes such as 1-octene, styrene, and allylanisole were readily hydrogenated within \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in quantitative yields using \(0.1{-}0.01\,\mathrm{~mol~}\,\%\) catalysts (entries 1-4 and 8-14, Table 1). At \(0.1\,\mathrm{~mol~}\,\%\) Co-loading, bpy-MOF-Co and bpyv-MOF-Co catalyzed hydrogenation of 1,1-, cis-1,2-, and trans-1,2-disubstituted alkenes in \(88–100\%\) yields (entries 15-25, Table 1). Additionally, dialkenes such as 1,7-octadiene, and tri-substituted alkenes such as \(\alpha\)-terpinene, trans- \(\alpha\)-methylstilbene and 1-methyl-1-cyclohexene were completely hydrogenated by mBPP-MOF-Co in excellent yields (entries 29, 31-34, Table 1; Table S10, SI). In general, the order of catalytic activity of MOF-catalysts in hydrogenation was mBPP-MOF-Co \(>\) mPT-MOF-Co \(>\) bpyv-MOF-Co \(>\) bpy-MOF-Co (Table S9, SI), due to the decreasing channel sizes in the series. TONs of \(2.0{\times}10^{5}\) and \(2.1{\times}10^{5}\) were observed for bpyv-MOF-Co and mPT-MOF-Co with 1-octene as the substrate (entries 5-6, Table 1), which are higher than those of previously reported Fe- and Co-functionalized salicylaldimine-based MOF catalysts. Remarkably, mBPP-MOF-Co displayed a TON of \(2.5{\times}10^{6}\) in hydrogenation of 1-octene, which is the highest TON that has ever been reported for Co-catalyzed olefin hydrogenation (entry 7, Table 1). In this case, a turnover frequency of \(1.1{\times}10^{5}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) per cobalt center was achieved in the first 9 hours, and after simple filtration, the \(n\)-octane product contained only \(0.26\,\mathrm{ppb}\) Co and \(1.34\,\mathrm{ppb}\) Zr. Moreover, mBPP-MOF-Co catalyzed hydrogenation of tri-substituted alkenes also proceeded efficiently under milder reaction conditions of 4 atm of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and 22 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Table S10, SI). MOF-Co catalysts are also tolerant of carbonyl group. Functionalized alkenes such as allyl acetate, dimethylitaconate and 2-vinylpyridine were hydrogenated selectively to propyl acetate, dimethyl-2-methylsuccinate, and 2-ethylpyridine, respectively, in good yields (entries 35-38, Table 1). MOF catalysts were also active in hydrogenation of tetrasubstituted alkenes, with a TON of 170 observed for the hydrogenation of tetramethyl-ethylene by mBPP-MOF-Co (entry 40, Table 1).
Impressively, at \(0.5\,\mathrm{~mol}\%\) Co loading, both bpy-MOF-Co and bpyv-MOF-Co catalysts can be recovered and reused for at least 16 times for the hydrogenation of 1-octene without loss of catalytic activity (Figures S40-42, SI). Complete conversion was observed in every run without olefin isomerization or formation of other byproducts. PXRD patterns of the MOF catalysts after catalysis were identical to those of the pristine MOF catalysts, indicating the stability of the framework under catalytic conditions (Figures 2d, S3, S6 and S12, SI). Additionally, ICP-MS analyses of the organic product showed a negligible metal leaching after the \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\) run, with the leaching of \(0.0004\%\) Co and \(0.001\%\) Zr for bpy-MOF-Co, \(0.0005\%\) Co and \(0.002\%\) Zr for bpyv-MOF-Co, and \(0.08\%\) Co and \(0.1\%\) Zr for mPT-MOF-Co, respectively. A "cross" test further confirmed the heterogeneity of MOF-catalysts: after completely hydrogenating styrene in \(6\,\mathrm{~h~}\), bpyv-MOF-Co was separated from the supernatant. An equal amount of 1-octene was added to the solid and supernatant, respectively. After 18 h under hydrogen atmosphere, 1-octene was completely converted to \(n\)-octane in presence of the MOF solid but no conversion was observed in presence of the supernatant. This
experiment proved that the MOF-Co, not the leached species, was the active catalyst for hydrogenation. Comparisons of the activities of MOF-Co catalysts with those of \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) activated with \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) and mercury poisoning experiments support that MOF-Co materials are truly single-site solid catalysts with activities several orders of magnitude higher than their homogeneous counterparts (Table S8, SI). In addition, mBPP-MOF-Co gave higher conversion of styrene compared to the analogous bulkier alkene, 4-tert-butylstyrene under identical conditions, which demonstrates that catalysis is facilitated by Co-sites both inside the pores and on the outside of the MOFs not the framework surface alone (Section 7.7, SI).
Stabilization of Bpy-Co(solvent)2 Species in the MOFs Is Responsible for Catalytic Alkene Hydrogenation. The catalytically active species generated after \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) treatment of \(\mathrm{MOF-CoCl}_{2}\) materials were investigated by comparison with homogeneous analogs using structural, spectroscopic, and computational techniques. Treatment of \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) with 2.0 equiv. of \(\mathrm{NaEt}_{3}\mathrm{BH}\) in THF under dinitrogen atmosphere at room temperature afforded a deep blue solution of \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\) (0.5 equiv) with the concomitant formation of one equiv. of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and Co nanoparticles as a black precipitate (Figure 3b). \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\cdot1/3\mathrm{THF}\) was crystallized from a concentrated \(\mathrm{THF}/\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution as a paramagnetic bluish-black solid, whose structure was determined by single crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure S16, SI). Presumably, the reductive elimination of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from the transient \(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy}\)-cobalt dihydride generates the reduced \(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy}\)-Co species, which undergoes intermolecular ligand-disproportionation to furnish the observed \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\) and Co nanoparticles. In a related precedent, the treatment of bulky aryl-substituted bis(imino)pyridine \((\mathrm{^ArPDI})\)-ligated Co-dichloride complex with \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) resulted in neutral Co-dinitrogen complex \((\mathrm{^PDI})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{N}_{2})\) via reductive elimination of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from the putative cobalt(II) dihydride intermediate. The steric protection around the Co center in \((\mathrm{^ArPDI})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{N}_{2})\) is key to stabilizing the reduced Co compound from undergoing intermolecular decomposition. \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\) showed broad UV-vis peaks in THF at \(635\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(688\,\mathrm{nm}\), indicating a strong metal-to-ligand charge-transfer (MLCT) transition in this low valence Co species. However, \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\) is EPR silent even at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\), possibly due to rapid relaxation processes.
Treatment of bpyv-MOF- \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) with \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) in THF gave bpyv-MOF-Co as a bluish-black solid which was characterized by EXAFS to contain \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{THF})_{\mathrm{x}}\) (93%) and a trace amount of Co nanoparticles (7%). The EXAFS spectrum showed contributions from both Co-N and Co-Co scattering paths, with their bond lengths fitted at \(1.96{\pm}0.04\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and \(2.45{\pm}0.05\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\), respectively (Figure 3c). These bond lengths are consistent with those of \(\mathrm{Co}^{(\mathrm{Me}^{2}\mathrm{bpy})}_{2}\) and metallic Co, respectively. The ratio for Co-N and Co-Co scattering paths was fitted to be 2:0.9, indicating \(\sim7\%\) contribution from Co nanoparticles (assuming an average coordination number of 12 as in metallic Co). We believe that the small amounts of Co nanoparticle formed from \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) trapped in the MOF at the metalation step. Control experiments ruled out significant contribution of catalytic activity from the trapped Co nanoparticles (Section 4.4, SI).
The spectroscopic, structural, and DFT calculation results indicate that \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}\) is likely the active catalyst for hydrogenation reactions. To further investigate the mechanism, the empirical rate law was determined by the method of initial rates \({<}10\%\) conversion), which shows that the hydrogenation of styrene by mBPP-MOF-Co has a first-order dependence on the catalyst and hydrogen concentrations, and a zeroth-order dependence on the alkene concentration (Figures 3f and S44, SI). Further DFT calculations were performed to propose a possible catalytic cycle (Figure \(3\mathrm{g}\) ). In the calculated mechanism, \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}\) first binds the alkene reversibly to form a \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bpy})\) (alkene) complex. Hydrogen then coordinates to the Co center to form a \(\sigma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) complex, which undergoes \(\sigma\) -complex  assisted  metathesis18  to  form  the \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{H})(\mathrm{R})\) species (Figure \(3\mathrm{g}\) ). Reductive elimination of \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{H})(\mathrm{R})\) in THF forms the alkane product and regenerates the \((\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}\) catalyst. Computation attempts on identifying the transition states gave a five-member ring transition states for the \(\upsigma\) -complex assisted metathesis step with a \(\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\dagger}=13.2\;\mathrm{kcal/mol}\) (Figure S38-S39, SI), while well-defined transition state of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding step was not found. Based on the kinetic and computational results, it can be proposed that the bpyCo(alkene) and bpyCo(alkene) \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) intermediates are in
pre-equilibrium with the \(\sigma\) -complex assisted metathesis as the turn-over limiting step.
