## Conclusions
We have developed highly active, robust, and recyclable single-site base-metal catalysts for alkene hydrogenation and hydroboration, aldehyde/ketone hydroboration, and arene C-H borylation reactions by simple post-synthetic metalation of bipyridyl- and phenanthryl-based MOFs. The MOF catalysts displayed unprecedentedly high turnover numbers (TONs) of up to \(2.5 \times 10^{6}\) and turnover frequencies (TOFs) of up to \(1.1 \times 10^{5} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for alkene hydrogenation. Structural, computational, and spectroscopic studies provide the first direct evidence for the stabilization of highly reactive and solution inaccessible (bpy)Co(THF)2 species in MOFs via active site isolation which shuts down intermolecular deactivation pathways. Computational, spectroscopic, and kinetic evidences further support a hitherto unknown (bpy\(^{-}\)Co\(^{1}\)(THF)2 ground state that coordinates to alkene and then undergoes \(\sigma\)-complex assisted metathesis with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to initiate catalytic cycles for alkene hydrogenation. This work thus demonstrates for the first time that MOFs enable an entirely new strategy for discovering base-metal based molecular catalysts. This novel strategy lifts the constraints placed on traditional homogeneous catalysis and can lead to practical earth-abundant metal catalysts for sustainable chemical catalysis.
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of MOFs and MOF- \(\mathbf{CoCl}_{2}\) precatalysts. In a typical procedure, \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) \(\left(10\ \mathrm{mg}\right)\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPP}\) \(\left(6\ \mathrm{mg}\right)\) and \(4,4^{\,\cdot}\)-bis(carboxyphenyl)-2-nitro-1,1'-biphenyl \(\left(14\ \mathrm{mg}\right)\) were dissolved in \(10\ \mathrm{mL}\) of DMF and \(0.1\ \mathrm{mL}\) of trifluoroacetic acid was added. The solution was heated at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 5 days to afford a pale yellow solid which was collected and washed thoroughly with DMF and THF \((17\ \mathrm{mg},\ 45\%\) yield). A THF solution of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) \(\left(6\ \mathrm{mg}\right)\) was added to the suspension of mBPP-MOF in THF, and the resultant mixture was stirred overnight, collected and washed with THF to afford the blue-green mBPP-MOF- \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\).
General procedure for MOF-Co-catalyzed olefin hydrogenation. To a suspension of mBPP-MOF- \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) \(\left(1.0\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%\ \mathrm{Co}\right)\) in \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) THF was added \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) \(\left(10\ \upmu\mathrm{L},\ 1.0\ \mathrm{M}\right.\) in THF), and the mixture was stirred for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\). The solid was then centrifuged, washed with THF twice, and transferred to a glass vial in \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) THF, followed by the addition of \(\alpha\)-isopropyl styrene \(\left(30\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.21\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)\). The vial was then placed in a Parr reactor which was charged with 40 bar of hydrogen. After stirring slowly at room temperature for \(35\ \mathrm{h}\), the pressure was released, and the MOF catalyst was removed from the reaction mixture via centrifugation and extracted
with hexanes twice. The combined organic extracts were concentrated in vacuo to afford pure 1-(3-methylbutan-2-yl)benzene in a quantitative yield.
General procedure for MOF-Co-catalyzed hydroboration of alkenes. To a suspension of mBPP-MOF- \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) \(\left(1.0\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%\ \mathrm{Co}\right)\) in \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) THF was added \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) in THF \((1.0\ \mathrm{M})\) and the mixture was stirred slowly for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\). The solid was centrifuged out of suspension and washed with THF twice. To the solid was added pinacolborane \((34\ \upmu\mathrm{L},\ 0.22\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and 1-octene \((48\ \upmu\mathrm{L},\ 0.31\ \mathrm{mmol})\). The resultant mixture was slowly stirred at room temperature for \(18\ \mathrm{h}\) until complete conversion of 1-octene as monitored by GC. The solid was centrifuged out of suspension and extracted with hexane twice. The combined organic extracts were concentrated in vacuo to yield the pure product \((52\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.216\ \mathrm{mmol},\ 98\%)\).
General procedure for MOF-Co-catalyzed C-H borylation of neat arenes. To a suspension of mPT-MOF- \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) \((3.0\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%\ \mathrm{Co})\) in \(0.5\ \mathrm{mL}\) THF was added \(20\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) in THF \((1.0\ \mathrm{M})\) and the mixture was stirred slowly for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\). The solid was centrifuged out of suspension and washed with THF twice and with \(m\)-xylene once. \(\mathrm{B}_{2}\mathrm{pin}_{2}\) \((54.8\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.216\ \mathrm{mmol})\) in \(4.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(m\)-xylene was added to the vial and the resultant mixture was transferred to a Schlenk tube. The tube was heated to reflux under nitrogen at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{d}\). The reaction mixture was cooled to room temperature and the solid was centrifuged out of the suspension. The liquid was passed through a short plug of celite and then concentrated in vacuo to give pure 5-(4,4,5,5-tetramethyl-1,3,2-dioxaborolan-2-yl)-\(m\)-xylene \((92\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.396\ \mathrm{mmol},\ 92\%)\).
